{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"01","chapter_title":"Productivity and Workflow Automation","title":"File Organization for Coursework","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting file organization for coursework to coursework file organization plan, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Build a course/module/task folder tree so all files for one assignment travel together.","Apply a `date_course_task_version` naming pattern that makes the newest file obvious at a glance.","Keep a second synced copy so a single device failure never destroys the only version of your work.","Justify each filing decision by the question it answers for a future reader: which file, which version, where."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation","cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/08"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_calibration"]},"content":"# File Organization for Coursework\n\n![Four-step workflow for this section: Artifact (resume before-after revision) feeds Evidence (rubric, peer note, or usability trace), which feeds a Decision (accept, reject, or modify AI/software output), which feeds Revision (document visible change and limitation). Concept tags below name digital citizenship, Excel data storytelling, resume evidence, GitHub Pages, and privacy and account safety.](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-01-file-organization-for-coursework.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nBy the end of a single semester a typical student touches several hundred files: drafts, datasets, screenshots, downloaded readings, rubrics, and exports from group tools. The cost of disorder is not abstract. It shows up as a submitted draft that turns out to be `final2` instead of `FINAL_real`, a missing rubric the night before grading, or a deleted-by-accident essay with no second copy. File organization is the unglamorous foundation that every later skill in this chapter (calendars, templates, automation) sits on top of, because automation amplifies whatever structure you already have. If your files are chaotic, a rename rule run across the whole folder amplifies the chaos.\n\nThis section teaches a concrete, repeatable filing system you can set up in fifteen minutes and reuse every term. The test of the system is simple: a stranger (or future-you in week 14) can open one folder and find the newest version of any assignment without asking a single question.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Build a course/module/task folder tree so all files for one assignment travel together.\n- Apply a `date_course_task_version` naming pattern that makes the newest file obvious at a glance.\n- Keep a second synced copy so a single device failure never destroys the only version of your work.\n- Justify each filing decision by the question it answers for a future reader: which file, which version, where.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student finishes a lab report at 11:50 PM, opens the upload page, and faces four candidate files: `lab.docx`, `lab_final.docx`, `lab_final2.docx`, and `FINAL_real.docx`. The modified dates all read \"today\" because the student saved, duplicated, and re-exported all four during the same late-night session, so they cluster to the same day. There is no way to tell which is the version with the corrected calculations short of opening all four and reading them. That two-minute panic is the exact failure a naming convention prevents.\n\nThe deeper point is that file names are a communication channel. A good name tells a reader the date, the course, the task, and the version without opening the file. A bad name forces a guess, and guesses under deadline pressure produce wrong submissions.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nFile organization is the practice of encoding three pieces of information into structure and names so that retrieval is fast and unambiguous: **what** the file is, **which version** it is, and **where** it belongs. Two mechanisms carry the load.\n\nThe first is the **folder hierarchy**, which answers \"where does this belong.\" A flat Desktop with 80 items forces a linear scan; a tree lets you navigate by elimination. The reliable pattern for school is three levels: course, then module or week, then task. Everything for one assignment lives in one leaf folder, so it can be backed up, shared, or zipped as a unit.\n\nThe second is the **file name**, which answers \"what\" and \"which version.\" Computers sort names alphabetically, so if you lead with an ISO date (`2026-05-28`), files sort into chronological order automatically. A name built as `date_course_task_version` then encodes everything a reader needs:\n\n| Part | Example | What it answers |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ISO date | `2026-05-28` | When; also forces chronological sort |\n| Course | `cat100` | Which class, so search finds it |\n| Task | `essay` | What the file is |\n| Version | `v3` | Which is newest, with no FINAL_real guessing |\n\nThese two mechanisms work because they front-load the decision. You name and file the moment you create, not later, so retrieval never requires reconstruction from memory.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is a real before/after for one student's CAT 100 files.\n\n**Before (flat Desktop, undescriptive names):**\n\n```\nDesktop/\n  final.docx\n  final2.docx\n  FINAL_real.docx\n  IMG_4471.jpg\n  syllabus.pdf\n  Downloads/\n    document(1).docx\n```\n\nIn words: everything sits loose at the Desktop top level, three near-identical \"final\" documents, a camera-named image, and a syllabus PDF, with one stray document inside Downloads and no per-course grouping anywhere. You cannot tell which `final` is newest, which class the syllabus belongs to, or where the essay image goes.\n\n**After (course / module / task tree with dated names):**\n\n```\nDocuments/\n  CAT100/\n    Module07-Productivity/\n      file-organization/\n        2026-05-28_cat100_essay_v3.docx\n        2026-05-28_cat100_essay_rubric.pdf\n        2026-05-26_cat100_essay_diagram.jpg\n```\n\nThree concrete changes did the work:\n\n1. **Folder tree by course then module then task** so one assignment's files travel together.\n2. **Name pattern `date_course_task_version`** so `_v3` is obviously newer than `_v2`, no `FINAL_real` guessing.\n3. **A second synced copy** in a cloud drive such as OneDrive (5 GB free), Google Drive (15 GB free), or iCloud Drive (5 GB free) so a dead USB stick cannot erase the only version. University-provided storage (institutional OneDrive or Google Workspace for Education) is often the safest second copy, because it has effectively large quotas and survives a lost personal device.\n\nThe improvement is not nicer wording. It is a structure another person, or future-you, can navigate without asking.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Set up the folder tree and rename one file\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You just downloaded the Module 7 essay prompt and it landed in Downloads as document(1).pdf. What is the FIRST thing to do before drafting?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Open it and start writing in Downloads\",\"Create the Documents/CAT100/Module07-Productivity/file-organization/ leaf folder\",\"Rename it to FINAL.pdf\",\"Wait until the end of term to file it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"File at creation. Build the course/module/task leaf folder first so the file has a home before you ever open it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Now you move the file in and rename it. Which name follows date_course_task_version and sorts newest-first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"essay_FINAL_real.pdf\",\"cat100 essay.pdf\",\"2026-05-28_cat100_essay_prompt.pdf\",\"prompt.pdf\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Leading with the ISO date forces chronological sort, and course/task/version tell a reader what and which without opening it.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Last step: create the second synced copy in OneDrive so one dead drive never erases the only version. Location, name, and backup are the three decisions, made before you click Save.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A folder named CAT100/essays/labs/2026/week7/drafts/final/ is ten levels deep. What rule does it break?\" explanation=\"Over-nesting is as hard to navigate as a flat pile. Three levels (course, module, task) is the sweet spot for a semester; deeper hides files.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It violates the three-level (course/module/task) depth guideline by over-nesting\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It uses ISO dates incorrectly\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is missing a version number\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing is wrong; deeper is always safer\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen you create or save any file, make three decisions before you click Save, in this order: where it belongs (folder), what it is plus its version (name), and how it is protected (backup).\n\nA well-filed assignment should pass three quick tests:\n\n1. **Location test:** Does this file live in its course/module/task leaf folder, not loose on the Desktop or in Downloads?\n2. **Name test:** Does the name lead with an ISO date and say course, task, and version, so the newest copy is obvious without opening it?\n3. **Backup test:** Is there a second synced copy (OneDrive, Google Drive, iCloud Drive, or your university's institutional storage) somewhere a single dead drive cannot erase? Free-tier quotas change frequently, so verify the current offering rather than trusting a remembered number.\n\nIf any test fails, the file is findable today only because you remember where it is, and that memory will not survive to week 14.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is filing later. \"I will organize at the end of the term\" never happens, and by then the modified dates are scrambled and the context is gone. File at creation, when you still know what the file is.\n\nA second error is over-nesting: ten folder levels deep is as hard to navigate as a flat pile. Three levels (course, module, task) is the sweet spot for a semester.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the system to a concrete week. You start CAT 100 Module 7 and download three things: the assignment prompt, the rubric, and a dataset. Before doing anything else, create `Documents/CAT100/Module07-Productivity/` and drop all three inside, renamed: `2026-05-25_cat100_module7_prompt.pdf`, `2026-05-25_cat100_module7_rubric.pdf`, `2026-05-25_cat100_module7_data.xlsx`. Now every artifact for this module has a home.\n\nAs you draft, each save increments the version: `_v1`, `_v2`, `_v3`. You never overwrite the prompt or rubric, so the source-of-truth files stay clean. When you finish, the leaf folder holds the full story of the assignment in date order, and you upload the highest version number with zero ambiguity.\n\nThe walkthrough shows the payoff: the decision cost is a few seconds at creation, and the return is that retrieval, backup, and submission all become trivial.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader treats their own file system as something to audit, not just live in. Open your busiest course folder right now and ask: could a classmate find my latest draft in under thirty seconds? If the answer is no, the structure has failed even though every file is technically present.\n\nA strong reader of file organization can answer four questions about any folder:\n\n1. Which file is the newest version of this assignment, judged from names alone?\n2. Where would a brand-new file for this task go, without thinking?\n3. If this drive died right now, where is the second copy?\n4. What naming or folder rule would prevent the last mistake I made?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact here is the folder structure itself, plus a short note explaining your naming convention. It is strong when a reader can reconstruct your filing logic from one screenshot: the tree shows course/module/task, the names show date/course/task/version, and the note states where the backup lives.\n\nA useful note sounds like this: \"I switched from a flat Desktop to a `CAT100/Module07/file-organization/` tree and renamed files as `date_course_task_version`. I rejected adding a separate `old_versions` folder because version numbers in the name already mark the newest file, and an extra folder would just hide history I might need.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same system from school to an internship. The course/module/task tree becomes client/project/deliverable; the `date_course_task_version` name still applies; the OneDrive backup becomes the company's shared drive with its access rules. What changes is the backup constraint: at work, a personal cloud copy of company files may violate policy, so the \"second copy\" lives only where the employer allows. Recognizing that the structure transfers but the backup rule must adapt is the sign you understand the system rather than just one folder layout.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the file organization for coursework packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-01-file-organization-for-coursework-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"course file organization system with naming convention\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the course file organization system.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same naming and backup rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the structure.\n\nAI features now offer to do this organizing for you: as of 2026, Copilot \"Suggested Rename\" in OneDrive and File Explorer \"AI Actions\" can batch-rename or reorganize files based on their content. Treat these like any bounded support move: accept what fits your convention, modify what does not, and reject anything you cannot verify, and note that such features change frequently, so check what the tool currently does.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the coursework file organization plan for CAT 100 07.01. A peer comment says they cannot tell which file is your latest draft from the names alone. Choose the next revision move (rename to date_course_task_version, restructure the folder tree, or add a backup) and state the evidence that would show it helped (e.g., a classmate finds the newest version in under 30 seconds).\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your filing system passes the location, name, and backup tests: every file lives in a course/module/task folder, every name leads with an ISO date plus course/task/version, and a second synced copy exists.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"],"stem":"You finished a CAT 100 essay. Which file name will still tell you what it is in three weeks?","options":["final.docx","document(2).docx","2026-05-28_cat100_essay_v3.docx","asdf.docx"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A name with date, course, task, and version (2026-05-28_cat100_essay_v3.docx) stays readable. 'final' and 'document(2)' lose meaning once a fourth version appears.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_vague_filename","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework"],"stem":"Which folder structure keeps one assignment's files together and easy to find?","options":["All coursework saved loose on the Desktop","CAT100 / Module07 / file-organization / (essay, notes, rubric inside)","One Downloads folder shared by every class","An Images folder holding photos from all five courses"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A course-then-module-then-task tree keeps related files in one place. Desktop dumps and type-based folders scatter a single task.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_desktop_dump","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework"],"stem":"Your only copy of a paper lives on a USB stick that just stopped mounting. What practice would have prevented losing the work?","options":["Renaming the file to FINAL before the deadline","Keeping two copies in two places, such as local plus OneDrive","Saving the file as a PDF instead of a DOCX","Storing the file in a deeper subfolder"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A two-copies-two-places rule survives a single drive failure. File format, depth, and a rename do nothing against a dead drive.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_no_backup","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework"],"stem":"You have lab1.pdf, lab2.pdf, and slides.pptx for one module. A classmate suggests an 'All PDFs' folder. Why is grouping by task usually better here?","options":["PDFs and PPTX files cannot share a folder","Grouping by task keeps the whole module together so nothing is hunted across type folders","PowerPoint files must always sit on the Desktop","File type folders load faster than task folders"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The lab PDFs and the slides belong to one module, so a task folder keeps them together. Type folders split one assignment across the disk.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_folder_by_filetype","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_file_organization_for_coursework_bounded_ai_move","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_file_organization_for_coursework","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"An AI organize-my-folder tool (such as Copilot Suggested Rename or File Explorer AI Actions) proposes renaming your CAT 100 folder to a date-leading scheme, padding versions as _v2, and auto-deleting files it labels 'duplicates.' Which response shows a bounded AI move?","options":["Accept everything, since the AI has read the file contents","Reject everything, since AI tools cannot help with file organization","Accept the date-leading pattern, modify the version padding to _v02, and reject the auto-delete because the tool cannot know which version has the corrected calculations","Accept the auto-delete first so the folder is smaller before renaming"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A bounded move accepts what fits your convention, modifies what is close, and rejects what you cannot verify. Auto-deleting 'duplicates' is the unverifiable step: the AI cannot tell which copy holds the corrected calculations.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_blind_ai_bulk_rename","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_desktop_dump","pattern":"everything_on_desktop","description":"Saving every coursework file loose on the Desktop or in Downloads instead of a course folder tree","trigger":"learner stores files like Final2_real.docx next to unrelated downloads with no folder per course","feedback":"Build a folder tree: CAT100 / Module07 / assignment, then save into it. A flat Desktop hides which file belongs to which task.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_vague_filename","pattern":"nondescriptive_file_names","description":"Naming files final.docx, document(1).docx, or untitled so versions cannot be told apart","trigger":"learner has Essay_final.docx, Essay_final2.docx, and Essay_FINAL_real.docx in one folder","feedback":"Use a name pattern like 2026-05-28_cat100_essay_v3.docx so date, course, task, and version are all readable at a glance.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_no_backup","pattern":"single_copy_no_backup","description":"Keeping the only copy of coursework on one local drive with no cloud or external backup","trigger":"learner edits directly on a USB stick or local folder with no synced copy","feedback":"Keep at least two copies in two places (for example local plus OneDrive). One drive failure should not erase a submission.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_folder_by_filetype","pattern":"organize_by_type_not_task","description":"Grouping by file extension (all PDFs together, all images together) instead of by course and task","trigger":"learner has one Images folder mixing photos from every class","feedback":"Organize by course then task so related files travel together. Type-based folders scatter one assignment across many places.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_1_blind_ai_bulk_rename","pattern":"blind_ai_bulk_action","description":"Trusting an AI bulk-rename or organize-my-folder action across a whole folder without checking which file is the real latest version","trigger":"learner accepts every AI rename and auto-delete suggestion in one click, including removal of files the tool labels duplicates","feedback":"Treat AI organize suggestions as a bounded move: accept what fits your naming convention, modify what is close, and reject anything you cannot verify, especially deletions, because the tool cannot know which version holds your corrected work.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"2359dfc1c4f3b8479e65dd274247c031602245b18e16f2ea9feedb5887c4973c","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}